==English==
{{wikipedia|Trooper (rank)}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|troop|er|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|troupe|lang=fr}}.

Sense of cavalry soldier attested 1640, mounted policeman 1858 (Australia), state policeman 1911 (US). The sense of "one who endures adversity" comes from English {{term|trouper||member of an acting troupe|lang=en}}, 1959, but through assimilation with the sense of "soldier" has come to be usually spelled "trooper".

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtɹuːpə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːpə(ɹ)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|military|lang=en}} A soldier of private rank in [[cavalry]] or [[armour]]. Abbreviated [[Tpr]].
# A [[cavalry]] horse; [[charger]].
# A [[soldier]].
# {{context|British|lang=en}} A [[troopship]].
# {{context|US|lang=en}} A [[state trooper]].
# One who endures adversity or hardship with an attitude of [[stoicism]] and [[persistence]].
#: ''He was a real '''trooper''' about taking care of the kids for the weekend.''

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|military}} [[cavalryman]], [[horse soldier]], [[crewman]], [[armoured soldier]], [[armored soldier]]
* {{sense|military}} [[zipperhead]] {{qualifier|Canadian military slang}}
* {{sense|person able to endure hardship}} [[survivor]], [[tough cookie]]

